## Q9. Schema Mismatch (S3 → Redshift)

“My approach is to fully normalize the schema in Glue before loading into Redshift. I don’t rely on Redshift to fix anything.

First, I define the target schema clearly—column names, types, precision, timezone handling.

Then in Glue:

* I rename columns to match exactly—usually snake_case and avoiding reserved words
* I cast all types explicitly—like string to bigint, decimal with correct precision, timestamps in UTC

For timestamps, I always normalize to UTC before loading.

I also handle edge cases:

* Strings like ‘Y/N’ → boolean
* Invalid casts → filtered or set to null

Then I run validation checks:

* Row counts
* Null ratios
* Bad cast counts

For loading, I always go:
S3 → staging table → MERGE into final table

This gives me idempotency and safe retries.

And if schema drift is expected, I enforce a mapping layer so unexpected fields fail fast instead of silently breaking downstream.

So basically, Glue becomes the schema enforcement layer—not Redshift.” 

